  "worked_solution": "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x)\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{\u2032} & ={u}^{\u2032}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x-u\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{\u2033} & ={u}^{\u2033}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x-2{u}^{\u2032}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x-u\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{\u2033}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x-2{u}^{\u2032}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x-u\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x)+u\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{\u2033}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x-2{u}^{\u2032}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{\u2033} & =2{u}^{\u2032}\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{\u2032}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{\u2032}=2w\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{sec}^{2}\u2061x.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{\u2032},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{\u2032}={c}_{1}{sec}^{2}\u2061x\\,\u27f9\\,u={c}_{1}\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x({c}_{1}\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x(\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x)=\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=1\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x+B\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x.\n$$",
